usamts-2019-r2-01.body:
For a square with vertices $a, b, c, d$ in clockwise order, $a + c$ and $b + d$ are the sums along its two **diagonals**. Each diagonal sum is the sum of two different numbers from $1$ to $16$, so it lies between $3$ and $31$. Factor each clue into two factors in that range:

* $650 = 25\cdot 26$ (the only option: $13\cdot 50$, $10\cdot 65$, … are out of range);
* $638 = 22\cdot 29$ (as $638 = 2\cdot 11\cdot 29$);
* $338 = 13\cdot 26$;
* $77 = 7\cdot 11$;
* $50 = 5\cdot 10$ ($2\cdot 25$ is impossible since a diagonal sum is at least $3$);
* $130 = 10\cdot 13$ or $5\cdot 26$.

The large sums $29$, $26$, $25$ in the upper right need large numbers there, while the small sums $5$, $7$ in the lower left need small numbers there. The diagonal sums $5 = 1 + 4$ or $2 + 3$ and $7$ leave very few options, and the $5$ in the bottom-right corner is already used. Trying the few possibilities for the lower-left squares and then extending through the $130$, $338$, $638$ and $650$ squares leads to the unique filling
$$\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|}\hline 14 & 13 & 15 & 9\\\hline 4 & 7 & 16 & 11\\\hline 3 & 1 & 2 & 10\\\hline 6 & 8 & 12 & 5\\\hline\end{array}$$
**Check** (diagonal sums): $638$: $(13 + 16)(15 + 7) = 29\cdot 22$; $650$: $(15 + 11)(9 + 16) = 26\cdot 25$; $50$: $(4 + 1)(7 + 3) = 5\cdot 10$; $338$: $(16 + 10)(11 + 2) = 26\cdot 13$; $77$: $(3 + 8)(1 + 6) = 11\cdot 7$; $130$: $(1 + 12)(2 + 8) = 13\cdot 10$. All of $1, \dots, 16$ appear once. (A computer search confirms this is the only solution.)

**Answer:** the grid above.
